      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "To analyse the environmental factors that contribute to Max’s Asperger’s syndrome and panic disorder, the film’s narrative provides several contributors to his disorders. First, Max’s panic disorder appears to have developed as a consequence of his underlying condition—Asperger’s syndrome. To be specific, his Asperger’s syndrome causes him to lack of ability to understand people’s emotions and express his emotions to others—which is the core symptom of his disorder. Therefore, based on the context that the film reveals, Max’s childhood is not compassionate and merely had people who understand him. For example, he mentions that when he was little, he needed a notebook to help him recognise various facial expressions—mainly angry and happy. The scene shows that while he was trying to recognise his mother’s facial expression, his mother just slapped his face—because he drew a nude woman on the wall. However, this depiction shows that his family is also dysfunctional and lacks support. The environment that he grew up in is not supportive enough, thus reinforcing his social isolation and intensifying his disability of emotional comprehension. When Max receives questions about relationships and love from Mary, his disabilities are intensified and trigger his feelings of confusion—leading to his panic attacks, as he can not explain through his logical thinking and he does not understand what is love. His long-term social isolation is the factor that makes him so anxious about questions of social interaction, because he lacks experience, and most importantly, he can not feel and understand. In addition, Max's panic disorder is also a result of difficulties in mediating emotions caused by his ASD. His Asperger's syndrome exacerbates his panic response, especially in terms of hypersensitivity to sensory stimuli, and he is more prone to panic episodes than normal."
